				<div class="post-694 post hentry category-net category-wpf">
			<h2 id="post-694"><a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/index.php/2007/10/wpf-controls-tabindex-moving-to-the-first-input-control/" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link to WPF Controls TabIndex: Moving to the First Input Control">WPF Controls TabIndex: Moving to the First Input Control</a></h2>
			<small class="meta">October 25th, 2007 </small>

			<div class="entry">
				<p>Here&#8217;s one of those real-world situations that no one bothers to demo.&nbsp; If you are building a form or have some sort of input your gathering from the user, you&#8217;ll likely want the first input control Focused initially.&nbsp; You have a few ways to do this (in ascending order of recommendation):</p>
<ol>
<li>Call Focus() on the first control explicitly (meh).
<li>Iterate the controls collection and find the&nbsp;lowest&nbsp;TabIndex&nbsp;(terrible <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code_smell">code smell</a>).
<li>Do the following in the Load event:</li>
</ol>
<blockquote><pre class="code"><span style="color: rgb(0,0,255)">this</span>.MoveFocus(<span style="color: rgb(0,0,255)">new</span> <span style="color: rgb(43,145,175)">TraversalRequest</span>(<span style="color: rgb(43,145,175)">FocusNavigationDirection</span>.Next));</pre>
</blockquote>
			</div>

